<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.5.2: Solutions Written as Square Roots</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02407</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.5.2: Solutions Written as Square Roots</md:title>
    <md:uuid>74f239fb-933a-4549-8255-7b47b1b282b2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Solve each equation. Use the notation when appropriate and express radicals in simplest form.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>13</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_2ad65777-d650-407b-af6a-6a39f1aecbad" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Enter the solution to the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00007"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00009">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Enter the solution to the equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">No real solution</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00014">So far in this activity you encountered radical solutions that were easy to simplify. Sometimes you will get radical solutions like <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>. Let’s review how to simplify more difficult radicals using perfect squares.</para>
      <para id="para-00015"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Look at the ways to factor the number under the radical.</para>
      <para id="para-00016">For example, factor 12 from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00017"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00018"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Choose the factorization of the number that includes a perfect square. Rewrite the number under the radical using that factorization.</para>
      <para id="para-00019"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00021"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Recall that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00023"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:msqrt><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Simplify the perfect squares.</para>
      <para id="para-00025"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:msqrt><m:mo>*</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msqrt><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msqrt><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00024">So <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msqrt><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00028"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00026">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00029"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00032"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00030">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00033"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00053"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00034">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00036"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math> (or equivalent)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Solving Quadratics with Square Roots</title>
      <para id="para-00037">Watch the following video to learn more about equations with solutions that are square roots.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5033df694da12da637450b921dc619cad59325d5"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00038">Solve <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>19</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Solving Quadratics with Radical Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00039">When solving quadratic equations, it is important to remember that:</para>
        <list id="list-00001">
          <item>Any positive number has two square roots, one positive and one negative, because there are two numbers that can be squared to make that number. (For example, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> both equal 36, so 6 and -6 are both square roots of 36.)</item>
          <item>The square root symbol <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mspace width="0.278em"/></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> can be used to express the positive square root of a number. For example, the square root of 36 is 6, but it can also be written as <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>36</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math> because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>36</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>36</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>36</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
          <item>To express the negative square root of a number, say 36, we can write -6 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>36</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
          <item>When a number is not a perfect square (for example, 40), we can express its square roots by writing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00040">How could we write the solutions to an equation like <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>? This equation is saying, “something squared is 11.” To make the equation true, that something must be <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00041">We can write:</para>
        <para id="para-00042"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>      or      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00043"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>      or      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00044">A more compact way to write the two solutions to the equation is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Solving Quadratics with Radical Solutions</title>
          <exercise id="exer-00006">
            <problem id="prob-00007">
              <para id="para-00045">Solve <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
              <para id="para-00046">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00007">
              <para id="para-00047">Here is how to solve this quadratic equation:</para>
              <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Make sure the quadratic is isolated on one side. </para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Take the square root of both sides.</para><para> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:msqrt><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify. </para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Solve the equation. For this problem, add 2 to each side. </para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>±</m:mi><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Simplify. </para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:msqrt><m:mrow><m:mn>17</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msqrt></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>